this morning a Russian judge denied bail  for an American being held on spying  charges the court session was Paul  Wieland's first public appearance since  he was arrested more than three weeks  ago Elizabeth Palmer was in Moscow where  Wieland's Russian lawyer talked about  the case and his clients response good  morning I'm standing in front of the  Moscow City Court which is where  Wieland's hearing was held although most  of it took place in secret in fact even  the representative from the US Embassy  wasn't allowed in we got our first  glimpse of Whelan when everybody  including the cameras was invited to  hear the judge's decision  Paul Whelan as as usual in Russian  courts stood behind glass in a sort of  prisoner's cage he looked relaxed even  though his Russian wasn't good enough to  understand what was going on and he had  to be briefed later by his lawyer and  his translator earlier the lawyer  vladimir nabokov had told me that when  Whelan was arrested he was in possession  of state secrets on a memory stick a  Russian website with close links to  Russia's security services is now saying  that those state secrets were in fact a  list of employees inside Russia's  Ministry of Defence with links to spying  women's lawyer told me essentially that  he's going to plead ignorance that Paul  Whelan had no idea that material was in  any way classified the lawyer also told  me that so far he's only seen a fraction  of the state's evidence he is going to  go ahead and appeal the no bail decision  but if that fails he said Paul Whelan is  looking at at least six more months in a  Russian jail for CBS this morning I'm  Elizabeth Palmer in Moscow
 

